# spike.fayth — the Spike persona: researches ONE question to a costed document, and
# carries no conversation.
#
# A fayth is a persona DEFINITION; an aeon is one summoned instance of it. Four things: a
# predicate carving its partition out of the graph, the memories it must read, the tools it
# may use, and what wakes it.
#
# WHY THIS PERSONA EXISTS AT ALL, given that the builder could do the reading. Feasibility
# research is the worst possible thing to do inside a long-lived session: it reads heavily,
# and almost nothing it reads is needed once the question is answered — only the conclusion
# is. Yet every page fetched stays in context and is re-read on every later turn for the rest
# of that session. Measured on the box this was written for: more than half of all context
# spend happened in turns already carrying 600k+ tokens, and a saturated session cost about
# sixteen times a fresh one per turn for identical work. A spike is exactly the shape of work
# that pushes a session into that band and then makes everything after it pay.
#
# A spike aeon starts at the floor, reads what it needs, writes one document, and exits. The
# conversation gets the document, not the reading.

FAYTH_NAME=spike
FAYTH_DESC="Spike — researches one question to a costed feasibility document"

# The partition. AND-labels, like every other persona: this can only ever see beads
# deliberately marked as spike work. The label is a configured key rather than a literal
# because it is read in four places, and a literal in four files is how four programs come to
# disagree about which beads exist.
FAYTH_LABELS="${SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL:+$SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL,}$SPIRA_SPIKE_LABEL"
FAYTH_EXCLUDE_LABELS="spira-poison,$SPIRA_ASK_LABEL,$SPIRA_CI_LABEL"

# Statutes only, as the builder reads. A spike executes no runbook.
FAYTH_MEMORY_PREFIXES="law-"

# NO FAYTH_REPO. The repository comes from the BEAD, through its `repo:<name>` label, exactly
# as it does for every other persona — a spike into one repository's problem is written up in
# that repository, and a spike with no `repo:` label is the home repo.
FAYTH_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# ONE. A spike is not throughput work: its output is a decision, and two aeons answering two
# questions at once buys nothing that answering them in sequence does not, while competing
# for the same account capacity as the builders who are actually landing code.
FAYTH_MAX_CONCURRENT=1

# LONGER THAN THE BUILDER'S HEARTBEAT WINDOW WOULD SUGGEST, and deliberately. Research is
# bursty: a spike can spend twenty minutes inside one fetch-and-read cycle producing no
# observable file change, which is precisely the shape the stall detector is built to end.
# The lease is what a reaper measures against, so it must comfortably exceed a long read.
FAYTH_LEASE_MINUTES=60
FAYTH_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=30
FAYTH_STALL_BEATS=15

# THE FULL TOOLSET, INCLUDING Bash AND Edit. The first draft of this persona withheld both so
# that a spike could not start implementing, and that was wrong in a way worth naming: for
# most interesting questions the only honest answer to "is this feasible" comes from trying
# it. A spike that may not build cannot tell "this is hard" from "I could not find out", and
# would report the second as the first.
#
# THE DISCIPLINE IS IN THE DELIVERABLE, NOT IN THE TOOL LIST. A spike is closed by its
# document, never by its code; a proof of concept is evidence for that document, kept on a
# branch of its own and named in it. A spike may leave a branch and must not leave a merge —
# and that is enforced by confine.sh at the landing gate rather than by withholding an editor.
FAYTH_TOOLS="Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,TodoWrite,WebSearch,WebFetch"
FAYTH_PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS=repo

# APPEND: the spike writes code proofs-of-concept, so the coding guidance stays.
FAYTH_SYSTEM_PROMPT=append
